  "id": "-0130-silk-roads-network",
  "kind": "process",
  "title": "The Silk Roads",
  "summary": "The Silk Roads were overland trade routes linking China with Central Asia, Iran and the Mediterranean. Han China officially opened trade with the west in 130 BCE after Zhang Qian's missions. From Chang'an the routes ran through the Hexi Corridor and the Jade Gate, split around the Tarim Basin, met again at Kashgar, and continued through Samarkand, Bukhara and Merv toward Syria. Silk, porcelain, paper and spices went west; horses, glassware, gold and silver went east, and Buddhism travelled along them. World History Encyclopedia ends regular use in 1453.",
